Chapter: CellStore
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 We say:

Section: Layout

 A sheet is one block: a 16-byte header, then rows times cols CELLS of eight
 bytes, row-major, then a text arena. The header holds the row count at 0,
 the column count at 4, the arena's capacity in bytes at 8 and the next free
 offset into the arena at 12, so a block answers its own bounds and its own
 remaining text room. A cell beyond the bounds reads as empty and is not
 written.

 DENSE RATHER THAN A MAP, and the margin is why. Measured 2026-09-08 on seed
 48F973C8 by docs/Probes/sheet-store-cost.codex: a 1,000-row by 26-column
 grid of 32-bit cells took 104,064 bytes, filled in 230 us and summed a
 column in 16 us, against 6,679,832 bytes, 12.4 ms and about 400 us for the
 same 1,000 cells in the tree's HamtMap keyed by `row,col` text. The trie is
 persistent, every set copies its path, and bare metal has no collector to
 give the old path back, so a sheet edited cell by cell grows without bound.
 The premise that dense storage costs megabytes is wrong at any column count
 a sheet shows: 104 bytes a row at 26 columns, and 208 now that a cell
 carries its kind.

 A cell is a KIND and a payload, four bytes each. The kind carries what a
 bare number cannot: an empty cell and a cell holding zero are different
 cells, and a label sitting in a column must not be summed as though it were
 a quantity. A number's payload is the value. A text's payload is a byte
 offset into the arena, where each entry is a 32-bit length followed by that
 many UTF-8 bytes.

 Eight bytes a cell rather than four is the price of the tag: a 1,000 by 26
 sheet is 208,064 bytes of cells against 104,064 before, plus whatever arena
 the caller asks for.

  cs-hdr : Integer = 16
  cs-rows-at : Integer = 0
  cs-cols-at : Integer = 4
  cs-cap-at : Integer = 8
  cs-next-at : Integer = 12

  cs-cell-bytes : Integer = 8

  cs-empty : Integer = 0
  cs-number : Integer = 1
  cs-text : Integer = 2

  sheet-new : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-new (rows) (cols) (text-bytes) =
    let blk = alloc-zeroed (cs-hdr + rows * cols * cs-cell-bytes + text-bytes) 64
    in let a = poke-32 blk cs-rows-at rows
    in let b = poke-32 blk cs-cols-at cols
    in let c = poke-32 blk cs-cap-at text-bytes
    in blk

  sheet-rows : Integer -> Integer
  sheet-rows (blk) = peek-32 blk cs-rows-at

  sheet-cols : Integer -> Integer
  sheet-cols (blk) = peek-32 blk cs-cols-at

  sheet-in : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  sheet-in (blk) (r) (c) = r >= 0 & c >= 0 & r < sheet-rows blk & c < sheet-cols blk

  sheet-off : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-off (blk) (r) (c) = cs-hdr + (r * sheet-cols blk + c) * cs-cell-bytes

 An ADDRESS, not an offset. Written as an offset it named absolute address
 1040 for every sheet in the program, so two sheets shared one arena outside
 either block and the second one's text overwrote the first's. A single
 sheet reads back whatever it wrote and cannot see the fault, which is why
 the arm below builds TWO.

  sheet-arena : Integer -> Integer
  sheet-arena (blk) = blk + cs-hdr + sheet-rows blk * sheet-cols blk * cs-cell-bytes

  sheet-text-used : Integer -> Integer
  sheet-text-used (blk) = peek-32 blk cs-next-at

  sheet-text-free : Integer -> Integer
  sheet-text-free (blk) = peek-32 blk cs-cap-at - peek-32 blk cs-next-at

Section: Cells

  sheet-kind : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-kind (blk) (r) (c) =
    if sheet-in blk r c then peek-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c) else cs-empty

 A payload is four bytes and `peek-32` answers them UNSIGNED, so a negative
 number stored and read straight back reads as its two's-complement twin:
 minus two came out as 4,294,967,294. A sheet holds negative quantities, so
 the payload is read as a signed 32-bit value.

  cs-sign-bit : Integer = 2147483648
  cs-two-32 : Integer = 4294967296

  cs-signed : Integer -> Integer
  cs-signed (v) = if v >= cs-sign-bit then v - cs-two-32 else v

  sheet-get : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-get (blk) (r) (c) =
    if sheet-kind blk r c == cs-number then cs-signed (peek-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c + 4)) else 0

  sheet-set : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  sheet-set (blk) (r) (c) (v) =
    if sheet-in blk r c then
      let k = poke-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c) cs-number
      in let p = poke-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c + 4) v
      in True
    else False

  sheet-clear : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  sheet-clear (blk) (r) (c) =
    if sheet-in blk r c then
      let k = poke-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c) cs-empty
      in let p = poke-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c + 4) 0
      in True
    else False

Section: Text Cells

 A text is APPENDED and never freed. A sheet edited cell by cell fills the
 arena, and `sheet-set-text` refuses rather than overwriting or truncating
 once the arena cannot hold the next string, so a caller can tell a written
 cell from an unwritten one. Reclaiming an abandoned entry needs a free list
 and is not this row's work.

  cs-write-bytes : Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cs-write-bytes (base) (at) (bs) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then 0
    else let p = poke-byte base (at + i) (list-at bs i)
    in cs-write-bytes base at bs (i + 1) n

  sheet-set-text : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Boolean
  sheet-set-text (blk) (r) (c) (t) =
    if sheet-in blk r c == False then False
    else let bs = text-to-utf8-bytes t
    in let n = list-length bs
    in let next = peek-32 blk cs-next-at
    in if next + 4 + n > peek-32 blk cs-cap-at then False
    else let base = sheet-arena blk
    in let l = poke-32 (base + next) 0 n
    in let w = cs-write-bytes base (next + 4) bs 0 n
    in let k = poke-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c) cs-text
    in let p = poke-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c + 4) next
    in let a = poke-32 blk cs-next-at (next + 4 + n)
    in True

  cs-read-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cs-read-bytes (base) (at) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else cs-read-bytes base at (i + 1) n (list-push acc (peek-byte base (at + i)))

  sheet-text : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  sheet-text (blk) (r) (c) =
    if sheet-kind blk r c /= cs-text then ""
    else let base = sheet-arena blk
    in let at = peek-32 blk (sheet-off blk r c + 4)
    in let n = peek-32 (base + at) 0
    in utf8-bytes-to-text (cs-read-bytes base (at + 4) 0 n [])

Section: Ranges

 A sum reads `sheet-get`, which answers 0 for an empty cell and for a text
 cell, so a label in a column of quantities contributes nothing rather than
 contributing its arena offset.

  sheet-col-sum-from : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-col-sum-from (blk) (c) (r) (acc) =
    if r >= sheet-rows blk then acc
    else sheet-col-sum-from blk c (r + 1) (acc + sheet-get blk r c)

  sheet-col-sum : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-col-sum (blk) (c) = sheet-col-sum-from blk c 0 0

  sheet-row-sum-from : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-row-sum-from (blk) (r) (c) (acc) =
    if c >= sheet-cols blk then acc
    else sheet-row-sum-from blk r (c + 1) (acc + sheet-get blk r c)

  sheet-row-sum : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sheet-row-sum (blk) (r) = sheet-row-sum-from blk r 0 0

